;; mova-edn-companion.mova -- a minimal `clojure.edn` workalike, written in
;; mova itself, so vendored Clojure test files that `(:require [clojure.edn
;; :as edn])` can load and run under mova.
;;
;; THIS IS TEST INFRASTRUCTURE, NOT PART OF THE SHIPPED LANGUAGE -- same
;; status as mova-test-shim.mova/mova-test-helper-shim.mova/mova-stacktrace-
;; companion.mova (see those files' own headers), and wired in by
;; tools/clojure-suite-run.bb the same way: `materialize-companion-clojure-
;; edn` (see that fn's own comment) writes this source, verbatim, as `(ns
;; clojure.edn)` plus this body, to `<scratch>/clojure/edn.mova` -- a real,
;; `require`-able module on mova's module path -- so `edn.clj`'s
;; `(:require [clojure.edn :as edn])` resolves instead of failing with
;; "could not locate namespace clojure.edn". Not spliced into anything (no
;; `clojure.test` shim names referenced) -- plain self-contained module,
;; same shape as `mova-stacktrace-companion.mova`.
;;
;; ONLY VENDORED CONSUMER at the time this was written
;; (`tests/clojure-suite/vendor/edn.clj`, confirmed by `grep -rn 'edn/'
;; vendor/` across all 51 vendored files): one call site,
;; `(-> o pr-str edn/read-string)`, inside two `defspec` generative-test
;; forms. `defspec` (`clojure.test.generative`) defines a var but the mova
;; `clojure.test` shim never calls it -- unlike `deftest`, nothing in this
;; suite's runner invokes a `defspec` var, so those two forms compile and
;; register but their bodies never execute (measured: 0 assertions ever run
;; from `edn.clj`, before or after this companion). This companion's job is
;; therefore honest-completeness, not score: a drop-in Clojure needs a real
;; `clojure.edn` namespace to exist and `require` cleanly, independent of
;; whether the one file that needs it happens to contribute assertions.
;;
;; API SURFACE IMPLEMENTED: `read-string` (1- and 2-arity, matching real
;; `clojure.edn`'s own arities). `read` (stream-based, `*in*`-defaulting) is
;; DELIBERATELY NOT implemented -- mova has no `*in*`/`PushbackReader`/
;; `java.io.Reader` abstraction anywhere in the codebase (confirmed by
;; grep), so there is no substrate to read a "stream" from at all, and no
;; vendored file calls `edn/read`. Per this codebase's own interop
;; principle (Rust-native semantics, JVM emulation only as deep as the
;; suite demands -- see `mova-stacktrace-companion.mova`'s header for the
;; same call on a different namespace), building stream plumbing nobody
;; exercises would be scope creep, not completeness.
;;
;; IMPLEMENTATION: a thin wrapper over mova's OWN `read-string` builtin
;; (`clojure.core/read-string`, `src/builtins/reflect.rs`), not a from-
;; scratch edn reader -- mova's reader already reads reader-conditional-
;; free Clojure data (bare `read-string`, without `{:read-cond :allow}`,
;; is exactly the mode edn needs: no `#?`/`#?@` dispatch), so re-deriving a
;; second, independent edn-only reader on top would duplicate the real one
;; for no measured benefit. `clojure.core/read-string` (fully qualified) is
;; used rather than the bare name because this namespace's own `read-string`
;; def below would otherwise shadow it for itself, recursing forever --
;; confirmed empirically (`(ns clojure.probe1) (defn read-string [s]
;; (clojure.core/read-string s)) ...` resolves the qualified call straight
;; through to the builtin even though the bare name is locally shadowed).
;;
;; MEASURED DIVERGENCES FROM REAL `clojure.edn` (oracle: 1.13.0-alpha6 via
;; `.oracle/deps`, transcript in `compat/edn-oracle-transcript.txt` --
;; every one of these is mova's underlying `read-string` being the FULL
;; Clojure reader, not a real restricted edn reader, so this wrapper
;; inherits all of them; recorded here for the deviations ledger, not
;; edited into DEVIATIONS.md by this file):
;;
;;   1. UNKNOWN TAGGED LITERALS: real `clojure.edn/read-string` on `#foo/bar
;;      300` (any tag not `#inst`/`#uuid` and not covered by a `:readers`/
;;      `:default` opt) throws `RuntimeException: No reader function for
;;      tag foo/bar`. mova's `read-string` passes the tag's payload
;;      through unchanged (`#foo/bar 300` => `300`) -- confirmed against a
;;      live mova build. This is the one divergence that would matter for
;;      `edn.clj`'s own `types-that-should-not-roundtrip` defspec (which
;;      expects `edn/read-string` to THROW on a non-ednable print-dup
;;      form), but as noted above that defspec's body never executes under
;;      this suite's runner, so it isn't exercised in practice.
;;   2. `#inst`: real edn reads it as a `java.util.Date`. mova's reader
;;      discards the `#inst` tag and returns the payload literally (`#inst
;;      "2020"` => the STRING `"2020"`, not a date/instant value) -- mova
;;      has no `java.util.Date`-equivalent value type to construct here
;;      (confirmed: no `Value::Date`/`Value::Instant` variant, only
;;      `Value::Inst` which is the UNRELATED record/deftype-instance
;;      representation -- see `src/value.rs`).
;;   3. NON-EDN CLOJURE READER SYNTAX real edn REJECTS but mova's
;;      `read-string` happily reads (all confirmed against a live mova
;;      build): `#"..."` regex literals (real edn: "No dispatch macro for
;;      \""), `#(...)` fn literals (real edn: "No dispatch macro for
;;      \("), `::kw` auto-resolved keywords (real edn: "Invalid token:
;;      ::kw" -- edn has no reader-time `*ns*` to resolve against), `#=`
;;      reader-eval forms (real edn: "No dispatch macro for ="). mova's
;;      `read-string` is the one, full Clojure reader (by design -- see
;;      `src/reader.rs`), so this wrapper is necessarily a permissive
;;      SUPERSET of real edn's grammar, never a stricter subset.
;;   4. `:readers`/`:default` OPTS: real `clojure.edn/read-string`'s opts
;;      map accepts `:readers` (a tag->fn map consulted before throwing)
;;      and `:default` (a catch-all tag handler). mova's `read-string`
;;      opts map only understands `:read-cond` (S5, unrelated to edn) --
;;      passing `:readers`/`:default` here is silently a no-op, matching
;;      "ignored, not wired up" rather than pretending to honor them.
;;   5. `:eof` OPT: implemented on a best-effort basis, NOT via true
;;      reader-level EOF detection (mova's `read-string` has no way to
;;      distinguish "reached end of input" from "read a literal `nil`" in
;;      its return value -- both are `Value::Nil`). This wrapper instead
;;      treats a `blank?` input string (nil, empty, or all-whitespace) as
;;      EOF and returns the `:eof` opt's value (defaulting to `nil`,
;;      matching real edn's own default) in that case, delegating to the
;;      real reader otherwise. Measured to match real edn on every case
;;      the oracle transcript covers (empty string, whitespace-only,
;;      custom `:eof` value) but would diverge on an EOF that real edn
;;      reaches only after skipping a `;`-comment-only input (e.g. `(read-
;;      string {:eof :EOF} "; just a comment")`) -- not itself blank, so
;;      this wrapper would call through to `clojure.core/read-string` and
;;      get real `nil` back instead of the `:eof` value. No vendored file
;;      exercises this corner (edn.clj never passes an opts map at all),
;;      recorded here rather than silently glossed over.

(defn read-string
  "Reads one object from the string `s`. Real `clojure.edn/read-string`,
  restated for mova's reader -- see this file's own header for exactly
  where the two diverge and why. `opts` (2-arity) is a map that may
  include `:eof` (value to return when `s` carries no readable form;
  defaults to `nil`, matching real edn) -- `:readers`/`:default` are
  accepted (silently ignored) for signature parity, not honored."
  ([s] (read-string {:eof nil} s))
  ([opts s]
   (when-not (nil? s)
     (if (blank? s)
       (:eof opts)
       (clojure.core/read-string s)))))
